; replhist.ax — the REPL's history: an in-memory ring, a file that
; survives the session, and the reverse incremental search Ctrl-R runs.
;
; This module OWNS NO TERMINAL and asks no question about one. Every
; entry point takes ordinary Int handles and Strings, and the single
; place interactivity enters is `histOpen`'s `interactive` argument,
; which the caller supplies. That is deliberate and it is what makes
; the whole subsystem gateable: `scripts/check-repl-history.sh` drives
; every behaviour below - persistence, the cap, dedup, multi-line
; round-trip, search, two writers at once - from ordinary programs with
; no pty anywhere, and can therefore assert BOTH directions (nothing on
; disk when history is off, exact bytes when it is on). A module that
; called isatty itself could only ever be tested in the off direction
; from a script, which is the vacuous shape this repository refuses.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FILE FORMAT, and why it is not one line per entry.
;
; `replMain` already builds a complete entry as ONE string with
; embedded newlines - `(strConcat (strConcat pending "\n") next)` at
; repl.ax:1495, handed to `replDispatch` at 1503 - because a form whose
; parens do not balance on one physical line keeps reading
; (`replParenDepth`). So a three-line `fn` is one entry, and a
; line-per-record file would take it back apart into three entries that
; each fail to parse on recall. The format has to carry the join.
;
; A record is:
;
;     <first line>\n
;     \t<second line>\n
;     \t<third line>\n
;
; The first physical line of an entry is written verbatim; every
; subsequent line is written with ONE leading TAB (byte 9); each
; physical line ends with LF. Decoding: a line beginning with TAB
; continues the entry in progress with exactly one TAB removed;
; anything else starts a new entry.
;
; The mapping is INJECTIVE, which is the property that matters and the
; reason for "exactly one":
;
;     entry "a\n\tb"  ->  "a\n\t\tb\n"  ->  entry "a\n\tb"
;
; so an entry whose own continuation line is tab-indented survives
; recall with its indentation intact. The precondition - that the first
; line does not itself begin with a TAB - is not assumed of the caller:
; `histNormalize` strips leading blanks from the first line, which is
; what `replTrim` (repl.ax:1489 and 1510) already does to every physical
; today.
;
; Decoding is TOTAL. A torn write, a file someone edited by hand, a
; leading-TAB line with no entry in progress - each yields some list of
; entries and never a failure, because the alternative is a REPL that
; refuses to start over a file it is not required to trust.
;
; Two entries were considered and rejected. A length-prefixed or
; escaped record (`\n` written as `\\n`) makes the file unreadable with
; `cat` and unfixable with an editor, which a dotfile people will look
; at should not be. A blank-line separator cannot represent an entry
; containing a blank line, which a multi-line `fn` routinely does.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHERE IT LIVES.
;
;   1. $AXIOM_REPL_HISTORY - the literal `off` disables persistence
;      entirely; an ABSOLUTE path is used verbatim; anything else
;      (relative, empty) falls through. The absolute test is the one
;      `replTmpRoot` (repl.ax:736-742) already applies to $TMPDIR, for the
;      same reason: a relative value would drop the file into whatever
;      directory the REPL happened to start in.
;   2. $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/axiom/repl-history, when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is
;      absolute.
;   3. $HOME/.config/axiom/repl-history, when HOME is absolute.
;   4. Otherwise "": the session keeps a ring in memory and writes
;      nothing.
;
; XDG_STATE_HOME is arguably the correct base for a history file and is
; DELIBERATELY not used. The only convention this tree has for
; user-level state is `run_repl` in scripts/check-repl-selfhost.sh,
; which sets `HOME="$work" XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$work"` with the comment
; "so a history file never touches the user's real one" - and those two
; variables are the whole of it (`grep -rn XDG` over the tree returns
; that one gate and nothing else; no `.ax` file reads $HOME at all).
; Reading XDG_STATE_HOME would escape that redirect and write into a
; developer's real home on every gate run. Adopting it later is one
; branch here plus one variable in `run_repl`.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; DEDUPLICATION, THE CAP, AND TWO REPLS AT ONCE.
;
; DEDUP is CONSECUTIVE only - readline's `ignoredups`. An entry
; byte-equal to the one immediately before it is dropped, from the ring
; and from the file. A full-history scan with move-to-end was rejected:
; it reorders history, and "Up N times goes back N entries" is the one
; thing a user knows about this feature.
;
; THE CAP is `histMaxEntries` entries in memory and after compaction,
; `histMaxEntryBytes` as the largest entry PERSISTED (a bigger paste
; stays in this session's ring but does not go into a file people read
; with `cat`), and `histMaxBytes` as the compaction TRIGGER. The
; trigger is a byte count and not an entry count on purpose: bytes are
; what another session's appends change, and are knowable from
; `sysFileSize` without decoding anything.
;
; TWO REPLS AT ONCE. Recording is write-through: one `sysAppendFile`
; call per entry, carrying the entry's whole record. Sys.ax's own
; comment states the property that rests on - "O_APPEND is what makes
; it atomic rather than merely equivalent: the seek to the end and the
; write are one operation, so two processes appending to one log
; interleave whole writes instead of overwriting each other's last
; one". So concurrent sessions interleave whole RECORDS and neither
; loses an entry. What is NOT promised: a session does not see entries
; another session appended after it started, because the file is read
; once at `histOpen`.
;
; Compaction is the one operation that rewrites the file, and it is
; guarded by a size compare-and-swap: read the size, read and decode
; the file, read the size AGAIN, and SKIP the rewrite if it moved.
; Compaction therefore carries forward everyone's entries, because it
; rebuilds from the FILE and not from this session's ring. The residual
; window is one syscall wide - an append landing between the second
; size read and the rename is lost - and is accepted rather than closed
; with a lock directory: with no `stat` in Sys.ax a process that died
; holding a lock would wedge compaction forever with no way to age it
; out, which is a worse failure than losing one line of history in a
; rare race.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. It does not read or write the terminal,
; print anything, or call `replOut`. It does not decide when an entry
; is complete - `replParenDepth` does, and history is handed the
; finished string. Rendering a recalled multi-line entry across several
; rows is the line editor's, and `histFlatten` is the one concession:
; the single row a Ctrl-R prompt shows.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE INTERFACE, in one place, because three other efforts call it.
; Everything is an Int handle or a String; nothing asks the caller to
; know how a `Hist` is laid out.
;
;   histOpen        (-> Int Hist)          interactive -> a session
;   histOpenWith    (-> Int Int Hist)      ... with an explicit cap
;   histRecord      (-> Hist String Int)   one COMPLETE entry; 1 kept, 0 dropped
;   histClose       (-> Hist Int)          compact if needed; 1 rewrote
;   histLen         (-> Hist Int)
;   histAt          (-> Hist Int String)   oldest first; "" out of range
;   histPath        (-> Hist String)       "" when nothing is persisted
;   histPersist     (-> Hist Int)
;
;   histBrowseUp    (-> Hist String Int)   Up; the String is the CURRENT
;                                          buffer, saved as the draft on
;                                          the first Up. 1 moved, 0 stuck
;   histBrowseDown  (-> Hist Int)          Down; 1 moved, 0 on the draft
;   histCurrent     (-> Hist String)       what the cursor points at
;   histBrowseReset (-> Hist Int)          leave browse mode; idempotent,
;                                          so an editor may call it on
;                                          every edit without asking
;
;   histSearchTop   (-> Hist Int)          where a fresh Ctrl-R starts
;   histFindBack    (-> Hist String Int Int)  needle, from -> index or -1
;                                          (a repeated Ctrl-R passes at-1)
;   histFlatten     (-> String String String) entry, separator -> one row
;
; The browse pair answers "did it move" rather than the text, and the
; text is read separately. A String return would need a sentinel for
; "no move", and "" is not available: the saved draft is legitimately
; empty whenever Up is pressed at a fresh prompt.
;
; `Hist` is spelled `Int` in every signature above, the way `Token` and
; `Diag` are throughout this tree.
;
; The codec (`histEncode`, `histDecode`, `histSerialize`,
; `histNormalize`, `histFlatten`), the three caps and `histResolvePath`
; are public as well, for the gate and for anyone who wants to read a
; history file without a session.
(import Mem)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(import Sys)

(import Err)

(import Path)

(import diag)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The three caps, spelled the way this language spells a constant.
;
; 1000 entries: bash's default HISTSIZE, and about 40 KB of ring for
; typical REPL lines.
; 262144 bytes (256 KiB): the compaction trigger. Big enough that a
; long-lived session never pays for it, small enough that the file
; stays something `cat` is a reasonable way to read.
; 4096 bytes: the largest entry written to the file. A pasted module
; is still recallable in the session that pasted it; it does not go on
; disk, where it would be a screenful of somebody else's source between
; two commands.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histMaxEntries Int)

(pub fn (histMaxEntries) 1000)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histMaxBytes Int)

(pub fn (histMaxBytes) 262144)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histMaxEntryBytes Int)

(pub fn (histMaxEntryBytes) 4096)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CODEC. No IO, no terminal, no filesystem - `restrict(no-io)` is
; the compiler refusing, not a comment, so a later "just peek at the
; file here" does not build.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; True when every byte of `s` is blank. A wholly-blank entry is not an
; entry.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: histAllBlank (-> String Int Bool))

(fn (histAllBlank s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    true
    (let ((b (strByte s i)))
      (if (|| (== b 32) (|| (== b 9) (|| (== b 10) (== b 13))))
        (histAllBlank s (+ i 1))
        false
      )
    )
  )
)

; Establish the format's precondition on the ENTRY rather than assuming
; it of the caller: no leading blank on the first physical line, and a
; wholly-blank entry answers "". `replTrim` already does this to every
; line the piped reader accepts (repl.ax:1489 and 1510); a TTY line
; editor may hand back an indented buffer, and the format must be total
; for whatever it hands back.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histNormalize (-> String String))

(pub fn (histNormalize s)
  (let (
    (n (strLen s))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< i n) (|| (== (strByte s i) 32) (== (strByte s i) 9)))
        (set i (+ i 1)))
      (if (histAllBlank s i)
        ""
        (strSlice s i (- n i))
      )
    }
  )
)

; One entry -> the exact bytes appended to the file. First line
; verbatim, every later line with one leading TAB, every line closed by
; LF. See the format section in the header for why the TAB and why
; exactly one.
;
; `histEncode ""` answers "\n", which decodes to no entries at all -
; the one place the mapping is not injective, and unreachable because
; `histRecord` drops an empty entry before it gets here.
;
; The fragment vector is `vecNew` and not `vecNewRef`, which is
; `catStrs`'s own callers' spelling (diag.ax:952 and 1077) and the
; right one here for a reason: the vector is transient and it holds
; STRING LITERALS ("\t", "\n") beside slices, and a `vecNewRef`
; container hands back a share of every element when it dies. The ring
; and `histDecode`'s output ARE `vecNewRef`, because those own
; `strDup`'ed entries that outlive the call that made them.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histEncode (-> String String))

(pub fn (histEncode e)
  (let (
    (parts vecNew)
    (n (strLen e))
    (mut start 0)
    (mut first 1)
  )
    {
      (while (<= start n)
        (let ((nl (strFindByte e 10 start)))
          (let ((end (if (< nl 0)
            n
            nl
          )))
            {
              (if (== first 1)
                (set first 0)
                (vecPush parts "\t")
              )
              (vecPush parts (strSlice e start (- end start)))
              (vecPush parts "\n")
              (set start (+ end 1))
            }
          )
        ))
      (catStrs parts)
    }
  )
)

; A Vec of entries -> the whole file's bytes. The inverse of
; `histDecode`, and the payload compaction renames into place.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histSerialize (-> Int String))

(pub fn (histSerialize v)
  (let (
    (parts vecNew)
    (n (vecLen v))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (vecPush parts (histEncode (vecGetStr v i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (catStrs parts)
    }
  )
)

; Push `e` onto `out` unless it repeats the entry already there.
; Consecutive dedup lives here so that a file written by an older
; build, or interleaved by two sessions, is deduped on the way in as
; well as on the way out.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: histPushDedup (-> Int String Int))

(fn (histPushDedup out e)
  (let ((n (vecLen out)))
    (if (&& (> n 0) (strEq (vecGetStr out (- n 1)) e))
      0
      (vecPush out (strDup e))
    )
  )
)

; Keep the LAST `cap` elements of `v`, as a fresh vector. Newest-wins:
; the entries a user reaches with Up are the recent ones, so an
; overflow drops from the old end.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: histKeepLast (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (histKeepLast v cap)
  (let ((n (vecLen v)))
    (if (<= n cap)
      v
      (let (
        (out vecNewRef)
        (mut i (- n cap))
      )
        {
          (while (< i n)
            {
              (vecPush out (vecGetStr v i))
              (set i (+ i 1))
            })
          out
        }
      )
    )
  )
)

; A whole file's bytes plus the entry cap -> a `vecNewRef` of entry
; Strings, oldest first, consecutive duplicates collapsed and trimmed
; to the newest `cap`.
;
; TOTAL by construction: a leading-TAB line with no entry in progress
; starts one (rather than being dropped, which would silently eat the
; tail of a torn record), an empty line closes whatever is open, and
; nothing here can fail.
;
; Every kept entry is `strDup`'ed by `histPushDedup`, so the vector
; owns NUL-terminated copies rather than shares of the file buffer -
; `strSlice`'s answer is not NUL-terminated and this vector outlives
; the read.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histDecode (-> String Int Int))

(pub fn (histDecode text cap)
  (let (
    (out vecNewRef)
    (n (strLen text))
    (mut start 0)
    (mut cur "")
    (mut have 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< start n)
        (let ((nl (strFindByte text 10 start)))
          (let ((end (if (< nl 0)
            n
            nl
          )))
            {
              (let ((line (strSlice text start (- end start))))
                (if (== (strLen line) 0)
                  ; a blank physical line closes whatever is open
                  {
                    (if (== have 1)
                      (histPushDedup out cur)
                      0
                    )
                    (set have 0)
                    (set cur "")
                    0
                  }
                  (if (== (strByte line 0) 9)
                    ; a continuation: exactly one TAB comes off
                    (let ((body (strSlice line 1 (- (strLen line) 1))))
                      (if (== have 1)
                        {
                          (set cur (strConcat (strConcat cur "\n") body))
                          0
                        }
                        {
                          (set cur body)
                          (set have 1)
                          0
                        }
                      )
                    )
                    ; anything else starts a fresh entry
                    {
                      (if (== have 1)
                        (histPushDedup out cur)
                        0
                      )
                      (set cur line)
                      (set have 1)
                      0
                    }
                  )
                )
              )
              (set start (+ end 1))
            }
          )
        ))
      (if (== have 1)
        (histPushDedup out cur)
        0
      )
      (histKeepLast out cap)
    }
  )
)

; A multi-line entry on ONE row, for the Ctrl-R prompt: every newline
; becomes `sep`. The separator is the caller's because the editor picks
; the glyph, and `style$visLen` measures an ASCII and a multi-byte
; separator alike.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histFlatten (-> String String String))

(pub fn (histFlatten e sep)
  (let (
    (parts vecNew)
    (n (strLen e))
    (mut start 0)
    (mut first 1)
  )
    {
      (while (<= start n)
        (let ((nl (strFindByte e 10 start)))
          (let ((end (if (< nl 0)
            n
            nl
          )))
            {
              (if (== first 1)
                (set first 0)
                (vecPush parts sep)
              )
              (vecPush parts (strSlice e start (- end start)))
              (set start (+ end 1))
            }
          )
        ))
      (catStrs parts)
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FILE. Everything below reaches the filesystem and says so:
; `;@axiom:effect(io)`, which AX3042 makes an error to omit.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The directory containing `p`, WITHOUT the trailing slash `pathDir`
; keeps.
;
; That slash is right for `pathDir`'s job - the answer is meant to be
; prefixed onto a name - and wrong for walking upwards, because
; `pathDir` is then IDEMPOTENT on its own answer: `pathDir "/a/b/"` is
; `"/a/b/"`, not `"/a/"`. Measured by reading Path.ax:70-86, and it is
; the reason `histEnsureDir` cannot simply call `pathDir` twice.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: histDirOf (-> String String))

(fn (histDirOf p)
  (let ((d (pathDir p)))
    (let ((n (strLen d)))
      (if (== n 0)
        ""
        (strDup (strSlice d 0 (- n 1)))
      )
    )
  )
)

; Where the history file lives, or "" for "no persistence". The
; resolution order is the header's; `interactive` 0 answers "" without
; reading the environment at all.
;
; Every answer is an ALLOCATED string - `strDup`, `pathJoin` and
; `strConcat` all allocate, and `strAlloc` zero-fills len+1 - because
; the caller hands it to `strCStr` and thence to a syscall. `sysEnv`'s
; own answer is a SLICE into the environment block with no NUL after
; it (Sys.ax:912-915), so `(strCStr (sysEnv ...))` would open a path
; nobody named: the value runs on into the next environment string.
; The gate greps this file for that spelling.
(pub :: histResolvePath (-> Int String))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histResolvePath interactive)
  (if (== interactive 0)
    ""
    (let ((v (sysEnv "AXIOM_REPL_HISTORY")))
      (if (strEq v "off")
        ""
        (if (pathIsAbsolute v)
          (strDup v)
          (let ((xdg (sysEnv "XDG_CONFIG_HOME")))
            (if (pathIsAbsolute xdg)
              (pathJoin (pathJoin xdg "axiom") "repl-history")
              (let ((home (sysEnv "HOME")))
                (if (pathIsAbsolute home)
                  (pathJoin (pathJoin (pathJoin home ".config") "axiom") "repl-history")
                  ""
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; Create the two directories above `path`, ignoring every failure.
; Answers 0 always.
;
; IGNORING IS THE POINT, and so is what it costs. `-EEXIST` is the
; normal answer and says nothing is wrong. Any OTHER failure - a
; read-only home, a name that is a file, a path more than two levels
; deep that does not exist - leaves this session with a resolved path
; whose every append silently fails, and `histPersist` still answers 1,
; because 1 means "there is a file to write to" and not "the kernel
; will accept the write". A REPL must not refuse to start over its
; history file, and there is no channel here to complain on: this
; module writes nothing to the terminal. The visible symptom is history
; that does not survive the session, which is the same symptom as
; history being off, and that is the trade this makes deliberately.
;
; The `(isErr (sysMkdir (strCStr d) mode))` spelling is `replEval`'s
; (repl.ax:808), applied to an ALLOCATED path for the reason above.
(pub :: histEnsureDir (-> String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histEnsureDir path)
  (let ((d (histDirOf path)))
    (let ((g (histDirOf d)))
      {
        (if (> (strLen g) 0)
          (if (isErr (sysMkdir (strCStr g) sysDirMode))
            0
            0
          )
          0
        )
        (if (> (strLen d) 0)
          (if (isErr (sysMkdir (strCStr d) sysDirMode))
            0
            0
          )
          0
        )
        0
      }
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SESSION.
;
; A `Hist` is an Int handle, the shape core.ax's `Token` established:
; a `pub struct` of word slots, one constructor, one accessor per
; field. Callers never touch the layout.
;
;   entries  the ring, a `vecNewRef` of entry Strings, OLDEST FIRST
;   path     "" when nothing is persisted, else an allocated path
;   cap      histMaxEntries, held per session so a test can lower it
;   pos      the browse cursor; `pos == (vecLen entries)` means "on the
;            draft", which is where a session that is not browsing sits
;   draft    the editor buffer saved when browsing began
;   persist  1 when a path resolved and this session will try to
;            write to it; see `histEnsureDir` for what it does NOT
;            promise
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct Hist
  (entries : Int)
  (path : String)
  (cap : Int)
  (pos : Int)
  (draft : String)
  (persist : Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: histRing (-> Int Int))

(fn (histRing h) (memGetWord h 0))

; The resolved history file, or "" when this session persists nothing.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histPath (-> Int String))

(pub fn (histPath h) (memGetWordStr h 1))

; 1 when this session writes to a file.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histPersist (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (histPersist h) (memGetWord h 5))

; How many entries the ring holds.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histLen (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (histLen h) (vecLen (histRing h)))

; The entry at `i`, oldest first, or "" out of range.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histAt (-> Int Int String))

(pub fn (histAt h i)
  (if (< i 0)
    ""
    (if (>= i (histLen h))
      ""
      (vecGetStr (histRing h) i)
    )
  )
)

; Build a session. `interactive` is 1 only when the caller has
; established that a person is at the other end; this module does not
; ask. 0 resolves no path, reads nothing and can write nothing.
;
; A cap is taken as an argument so a gate can drive the trim without
; recording a thousand entries. `histOpen` is the ordinary spelling.
(pub :: histOpenWith (-> Int Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histOpenWith interactive cap)
  (let ((path (histResolvePath interactive)))
    (if (== (strLen path) 0)
      (Hist vecNewRef "" cap 0 "" 0)
      {
        (histEnsureDir path)
        (let ((v (histDecode (sysReadFile (strCStr path)) cap)))
          (Hist v path cap (vecLen v) "" 1)
        )
      }
    )
  )
)

(pub :: histOpen (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histOpen interactive) (histOpenWith interactive histMaxEntries))

; Leave browse mode: the cursor goes back to the draft position and the
; saved draft is dropped. Called on submit, and by the editor on any
; edit that makes the buffer no longer a recalled entry, so that the
; next Up starts from the newest again.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histBrowseReset (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (histBrowseReset h)
  {
    (memSetWord h 3 (histLen h))
    (memSetWord h 4 "")
    0
  }
)

; Record ONE COMPLETE entry - the whole multi-line string `replMain`
; hands `replDispatch`, not a physical line. Answers 1 when it was
; recorded and 0 when it was dropped (empty, or a repeat of the entry
; before it).
;
; Write-through: the file gets this entry NOW, in one `sysAppendFile`
; call, so `:quit`'s `sysExitWith` and a Ctrl-C at the prompt both lose
; nothing. An over-large entry stays in the ring and is not written.
(pub :: histRecord (-> Int String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histRecord h raw)
  (let ((e (histNormalize raw)))
    (if (== (strLen e) 0)
      {
        (histBrowseReset h)
        0
      }
      (let ((v (histRing h)))
        (let ((n (vecLen v)))
          (if (&& (> n 0) (strEq (vecGetStr v (- n 1)) e))
            {
              (histBrowseReset h)
              0
            }
            {
              (vecPush v (strDup e))
              (memSetWord h 0 (histKeepLast v (memGetWord h 2)))
              (histBrowseReset h)
              (if (&& (== (histPersist h) 1) (<= (strLen e) histMaxEntryBytes))
                (if (isErr (sysAppendFile (strCStr (histPath h)) (histEncode e)))
                  0
                  0
                )
                0
              )
              1
            }
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; Compact the file if it has outgrown `histMaxBytes`, then answer 1
; when it was rewritten and 0 when it was not.
;
; THE SEQUENCE IS THE CONCURRENCY ARGUMENT. size, load, decode, size
; AGAIN - and skip when the two sizes differ, because another REPL
; appended while this one was reading and the rewrite would drop
; whatever it wrote. Compaction rebuilds from the FILE and not from
; this session's ring, so it carries forward every session's entries
; rather than replacing the file with one process's view of it.
;
; The rewrite is write-to-temp then `sysRename`, which Sys.ax:458
; states is atomic within a filesystem: a concurrent reader sees the
; whole old file or the whole new one, never a half-written prefix. The
; temp name carries `sysGetPid` rendered by `diag$decStr` - NOT
; `driver$fmtIntStr`, which renders 0, 1, 2 and 3 and answers "1" for
; everything else, and which is exactly why every REPL on this machine
; once wrote to one scratch name (repl.ax:752-766).
(pub :: histClose (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (histClose h)
  (if (== (histPersist h) 0)
    0
    (let ((cp (strCStr (histPath h))))
      (let ((s0 (unwrapOr (sysFileSize cp) 0)))
        (if (<= s0 histMaxBytes)
          0
          (let ((v (histDecode (sysReadFile cp) (memGetWord h 2))))
            (let ((s1 (unwrapOr (sysFileSize cp) (- 0 1))))
              (if (!= s1 s0)
                0
                (let ((tmp (strConcat (strConcat (histPath h) ".tmp-") (decStr sysGetPid))))
                  (if (isErr (sysWriteFile (strCStr tmp) (histSerialize v)))
                    0
                    (if (isErr (sysRename (strCStr tmp) cp))
                      0
                      1
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; BROWSING - the Up and Down arrows.
;
; The pair answers "did the cursor move", 1 or 0, and the text is read
; separately with `histCurrent`. A String return would have to signal
; "no move" with some sentinel, and "" is not available: the saved
; draft is legitimately empty whenever Up is pressed on a fresh prompt.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Up. `cur` is the editor's buffer right now, saved as the draft the
; first time browsing begins so that Down can come back to it. Answers
; 0 at the oldest entry, where the editor should do nothing.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histBrowseUp (-> Int String Int))

(pub fn (histBrowseUp h cur)
  (let ((pos (memGetWord h 3)))
    (if (<= pos 0)
      0
      {
        (if (>= pos (histLen h))
          (memSetWord h 4 (strDup cur))
          0
        )
        (memSetWord h 3 (- pos 1))
        1
      }
    )
  )
)

; Down. Answers 0 when already on the draft.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histBrowseDown (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (histBrowseDown h)
  (let ((pos (memGetWord h 3)))
    (if (>= pos (histLen h))
      0
      {
        (memSetWord h 3 (+ pos 1))
        1
      }
    )
  )
)

; The text the browse cursor points at: the entry at `pos`, or the
; saved draft once the cursor is back past the newest entry. A recalled
; multi-line entry comes back as the ONE string it was recorded as,
; newlines and all - the editor renders it as several rows and, on
; submit, hands the whole thing back, where it dispatches in one go
; because it balanced when it was recorded.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histCurrent (-> Int String))

(pub fn (histCurrent h)
  (let ((pos (memGetWord h 3)))
    (if (>= pos (histLen h))
      (memGetWordStr h 4)
      (histAt h pos)
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; REVERSE INCREMENTAL SEARCH - Ctrl-R.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The index a fresh Ctrl-R starts from: the newest entry, or -1 for an
; empty ring.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: histSearchTop (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (histSearchTop h) (- (histLen h) 1))

; The greatest index <= `from` whose entry CONTAINS `needle`, or -1.
;
; `strFind` searches the whole entry string, so a needle on the third
; line of a multi-line entry matches - which is the behaviour a user
; typing a fragment of a `fn` body expects, and the reason the ring
; holds whole entries rather than first lines. `strFind`'s documented
; empty-needle rule (Str.ax:599-603) gives readline's other behaviour for
; free: Ctrl-R with nothing typed yet matches the most recent entry.
;
; A repeated Ctrl-R passes `(- at 1)`, so stepping is the caller's and
; this stays a pure function of (ring, needle, from). Case-SENSITIVE:
; the corpus is Axiom source and its identifiers are case-sensitive.
; `no-alloc` came off on 2026-08-31, TRANSITIVELY: this body allocates
; nothing itself, and `strFind` - which it calls once per ring entry -
; answers `(Option Int)` and allocates the `Some`. The claim held only
; while a constructor application was invisible to the effect row
; (`MM-EXEC-9a`), which is the same reason `strFind` lost its own.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: histFindBack (-> Int String Int Int))

(pub fn (histFindBack h needle from)
  (let ((n (histLen h)))
    (let (
      (mut i (if (>= from n)
        (- n 1)
        from
      ))
      (mut found (- 0 1))
    )
      {
        (while (&& (< found 0) (>= i 0))
          {
            (match (strFind (histAt h i) needle 0)
              ((Some _) (set found i))
              ((None) 0)
            )
            (set i (- i 1))
          })
        found
      }
    )
  )
)
